PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation confirms consensus on the diagnosis of Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis resulting from sequential antiepileptic therapy (valproate, topiramate, and lamotrigine). The debate centers on defining the specific initiating biochemical reaction: Phase II glucuronidation (specifically its inhibition by valproate, causing drug accumulation) versus Phase I cytochrome P450-catalyzed epoxidation (generating the reactive arene oxide intermediate that haptenates epidermal proteins). The optimal group resolution designates cytochrome P450-mediated epoxidation as the definitive generative biochemical reaction responsible for neoantigen formation and blister development, while framing the inhibition of glucuronidation as the upstream pharmacokinetic trigger.
}

